PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current consensus: The lab’s mistake was treating an invalid QC “pass” as proof that Batch 3 PDA contained active chloramphenicol and was safe. The B. subtilis 6633 QC culture was from a Passage 5 ATCC stick and had been repassaged weekly for 6 weeks, effectively well beyond acceptable passage limits and old by the June 28 experiment. It was likely nonviable or predominantly dormant spores, especially without a heat-shock/viability control. Therefore, no growth on PDA with chloramphenicol was not valid evidence of antibiotic activity—it could simply mean the QC organism was dead, dormant, or unsuited to the conditions.

The real cause of bacterial colonies only in Batch 3: chloramphenicol was added before autoclaving at 121°C for 25 minutes, which likely inactivated/degraded the antibiotic. Batches 1 and 2 had active chloramphenicol. When the bottles were left open from 7 am to 1 pm, airborne spore-forming Gram-positive rods, likely Bacillus spores, contaminated the PDA. Only Batch 3 lacked selective antibacterial activity, so those organisms grew.

Key error: equating “no growth” on the B. subtilis QC plate with proof that chloramphenicol was active and Batch 3 was safe. A proper QC would need a positive growth control on non-selective medium to prove inoculum viability, and possibly fungal growth-promotion controls because PDA is a yeast/mold medium. Some colleagues also note the QC may not have specifically tested Batch 3, and that B. subtilis may not grow well on PDA at 25.5°C.

Unresolved/nuances: exact QC setup and whether Batch 3 itself was tested; degree of chloramphenicol inactivation by autoclaving; exact passage count/storage age; and whether a viable B. subtilis inoculum would grow on PDA at 25.5°C. No major counterarguments emerged—only variations in mechanism emphasis: over-passaged/nonviable inoculum, spore dormancy, wrong QC organism/medium, and failure to include viability controls.
}

